foundation.question_text:
Which angle relationship is shown?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(2.4,0); \draw (0,0)--(0,2.4);
\coordinate (A) at (0.35,0); \coordinate (B) at (0,0.35);
\draw (A)--(0.35,0.35)--(B);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Name the relationship shown by the highlighted half-turn.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2.5,0)--(2.5,0); \draw (0,0)--(65:2.1);
\node at (-1.0,0.65) {$a$}; \node at (1.0,0.65) {$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

What relationship do the three sectors show?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(2,0); \draw (0,0)--(110:2); \draw (0,0)--(225:2);
\node at (0.55,0.8) {$p$}; \node at (-0.85,0.35) {$q$}; \node at (0.15,-0.9) {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Does the marked pair form a right angle, a straight line or angles at a point?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(2.3,0); \draw (0,0)--(0,2.3); \draw (0,0)--(38:2);
\node at (1.05,0.32) {$u$}; \node at (0.42,1.05) {$v$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Classify the relationship between angles $m$ and $n$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2.4,0)--(2.4,0); \draw (0,0)--(125:2);
\node at (-0.8,0.75) {$m$}; \node at (0.95,0.48) {$n$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A full turn has been split into four sectors. Name the relationship.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(2,0); \draw (0,0)--(80:2); \draw (0,0)--(175:2); \draw (0,0)--(270:2);
\node at (0.7,0.55) {$w$}; \node at (-0.55,0.7) {$x$}; \node at (-0.65,-0.55) {$y$}; \node at (0.65,-0.55) {$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
Which total belongs to the marked angles: $90^{\circ}$, $180^{\circ}$ or $360^{\circ}$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]\usetikzlibrary{angles}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0); \coordinate (A) at (17:2.3); \coordinate (B) at (107:2.3); \coordinate (C) at (58:2.1);
\draw (O)--(A); \draw (O)--(B); \draw (O)--(C); \pic[draw,angle radius=0.42cm]{right angle=A--O--B};
\node at (37.5:1.0) {$c$}; \node at (82.5:1.0) {$d$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The shaded path follows one side of a ruler edge. Which angle relationship is represented?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw[very thick] (-2.5,0)--(2.5,0); \draw (0,0)--(105:2);
\node at (-0.85,0.65) {$s$}; \node at (0.8,0.65) {$t$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Three paths meet at a playground post. Which relationship describes all the sectors around the post?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt); \draw (0,0)--(15:2.2); \draw (0,0)--(145:2.2); \draw (0,0)--(255:2.2);
\node at (0.45,0.85) {$1$}; \node at (-0.85,-0.1) {$2$}; \node at (0.55,-0.75) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Are the marked angles on a straight line? Explain from the diagram.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2.2,0)--(0,0)--(2.0,0.8); \draw (0,0)--(75:2);
\node at (-0.8,0.55) {$g$}; \node at (0.75,0.75) {$h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A square tile and two triangular tiles meet at a corner. Which known total should be used for every sector around that corner?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle; \draw (0,0)--(-1.5,1.2)--(0,2); \draw (0,0)--(-1.4,-1.3)--(2,0);
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The two labelled sectors share a vertex, but do they form a right angle?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(2.3,0); \draw (0,0)--(100:2.3); \draw (0,0)--(45:2);
\node at (1.0,0.4) {$j$}; \node at (0.35,1.0) {$k$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.question_text:
A learner says the three labelled sectors total $180^{\circ}$ because two arms form a line. Is the claim correct?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2.4,0)--(2.4,0); \draw (0,0)--(70:2); \draw (0,0)--(235:2);
\node at (-0.9,0.55) {$a$}; \node at (0.75,0.6) {$b$}; \node at (-0.1,-0.8) {$c$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two straight paths cross. State one $180^{\circ}$ relationship visible in the figure.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2.2,-1.0)--(2.2,1.0); \draw (-2.2,1.0)--(2.2,-1.0);
\node at (0,0.85) {$p$}; \node at (0.9,0) {$q$}; \node at (0,-0.85) {$r$}; \node at (-0.9,0) {$s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Choose the smallest known total that contains sectors $A$, $B$ and $C$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(2.2,0); \draw (0,0)--(90:2.2); \draw (0,0)--(190:2.2); \draw (0,0)--(280:2.2);
\node at (0.75,0.65) {$A$}; \node at (-0.65,0.7) {$B$}; \node at (-0.6,-0.55) {$C$}; \node at (0.75,-0.45) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
